      "text": "We start by analyzing the given recurrence relation \\( a_n = a_{n-1} a_{n+1} \\). This means that each term in the sequence depends on its two previous terms and the next term. To understand the pattern, let's consider what would happen if we repeatedly apply the relation to the known terms \\(a_1\\) and \\(a_{1776}\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\na_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7}\n\\]\n\\[\na_{1776} = 13 + \\sqrt{7}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(a_2\\). Using the initial condition and expressing the relation in terms of \\(a_1\\) and \\(a_2\\):\n\\[\na_2 = \\frac{a_1}{a_1} = 1\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, by applying the relation for \\(a_3\\):\n\\[\na_3 = \\frac{a_2}{a_2} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis suggests a periodicity in the sequence with a period of 2. To verify this, we can write \\(a_n\\) for \\(n = 1\\) and \\(n = 2\\):\n\\[\na_3 = \\frac{a_2}{a_2} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_4 = \\frac{a_3}{a_3} = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(a_1, a_2, a_3, a_4\\) all take the value 1. Given the periodic pattern \\(a_1 = a_3 = a_5 = \\cdots = 1\\), and \\(a_2 = 1\\) and \\(a_4 = 1\\), the sequence repeats every 4 terms.\n\nNow, we need to determine if this periodicity extends beyond \\(a_{1776}\\). Since 1776 is even and periodic in every 4 steps, the next term would be:\n\\[\na_{1777} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_{1778} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_{1779} = 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_{1780} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(a_1\\) and \\(a_2\\) repeat every 4 steps, and it remains consistent to conclude that the values of \\(a_n\\) for \\(n\\) in arbitrary intervals of length \\(\\lfloor \\frac{1776}{4} \\rfloor\\) are the same as the \\(4\\)-step progression.\n\nGiven that \\(a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7}\\), \\(a_2 = 1\\), \\(a_3 = 1\\), and \\(a_4 = 1\\), and knowing \\(a_{1776} = 13 + \\sqrt{7}\\), it should follow that \\(a_{1777} = 1\\), \\(a_{1778} = 1\\), \\(a_{1779} = 1\\), and \\(a_{1780} = 1\\) structurally from the periodic nature.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\\[\na_{2009} = a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7}\n\\]\n\nThe answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1 + \\sqrt{7}}\n\\]",
